design.decidesAndAsks:
It never asks. Every part is a read, so there is nothing to confirm.

Which tool: the descriptions carry it. fleet_status with health on for questions about every Spark ("is any Spark unhealthy", "is the hardware OK"). spark_diagnostics for one Spark, for a Spark that is dark, and for "how hot" or "how worn". Sparkie picks neither limits nor causes.

Code decides what is wrong, from one table of constants (HEALTH_LIMITS). Each is a starting guess from forum reports, so every line shows its number, its limit and its time:
- hottest zone at or above 90 C: hot;
- hottest zone at or above 60 C with the GPU under 10 percent and load under 2: hot while idle;
- disk at or above the drive's own warning temperature (70 C when the drive gives none);
- any of hw_slowdown, hw_thermal_slowdown, hw_power_brake_slowdown, sw_thermal_slowdown active: throttled;
- GPU at or above 80 percent busy with its clock under 1000 MHz: clock held low;
- wear at or above 80 percent, any drive warning flag, spare at or below its threshold, or any media error;
- any boot in 14 days that began with no shutdown record before it;
- any kernel hardware-error line in 14 days, counted by class: Xid, hardware error or machine check, PCIe AER, NVMe timeout or reset, disk I/O error, critical temperature;
- a dark period of 90 seconds or more that ended in a restart, with a hot last reading.

Code also picks the verdict word. "Needs a look": any line over. "Healthy": every line read and under its limit. "Healthy as far as I can read": nothing over, with the unread signals named. "Not checked": the Spark did not answer in 12 seconds. fleet_status without health on gives readings only and no verdict word, so partial data never reads as healthy.

For each dark period code picks one finding: still dark; never went down (it came back without restarting); restarted with a hot last reading; restarted with a cool one. When the Spark answers, it adds whether that boot has a shutdown record: present, none, or cannot read.

What it says when it cannot read something: the signal's name, "cannot read", the reason in a few words, and the command the owner can run to see it by hand. Fan speed is shown when the Spark exposes it and never decides a verdict, because owners report it missing or wrong on some units.

It applies no fix. Advice is fixed text keyed by the finding, never written by the model, and each line says whether NVIDIA or other owners are the source. It says "matches" or "consistent with", never "caused by".
